>
<main><section class="case-hero section-shell reveal"><div><span class="eyebrow">Case study 01 · Aviation product</span><h1>Reframing a mission-critical baggage platform.</h1><p>Discovery, MVP scoping and roadmap definition for the rebuild of a Baggage Reconciliation System supporting operations at Dubai International Airport.</p></div><div class="case-facts"><div><span>Role</span><strong>Technical Product Owner</strong></div><div><span>Environment</span><strong>Enterprise aviation operations</strong></div><div><span>Scale</span><strong>82M+ bags annually at DXB</strong></div><div><span>Delivery</span><strong>2 Scrum teams</strong></div></div></section><section class="case-body section-shell"><aside class="case-nav"><a href="#context">Context</a><a href="#problem">Problem</a><a href="#approach">Approach</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a><a href="#illustration">Illustration</a><a href="#ownership">Ownership</a><a href="#public-context">Public context</a><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a><a class="back-link" href="/#work">← Back to work</a></aside><div><section class="case-section" id="context"><span class="eyebrow">Context</span><h2>A critical operational system with a long horizon.</h2><p>The Baggage Reconciliation System sits inside airport operations where product decisions have operational consequences, dependencies across multiple teams and limited tolerance for ambiguity. My role was to translate operating needs into a structured product direction while coordinating delivery across two Scrum teams.</p></section><section class="case-section" id="problem"><span class="eyebrow">Problem framing</span><h2>Do not start with a backlog. Start with the operating model.</h2><p>A rebuild at this scale cannot be treated as a list of feature requests. The useful work was to establish which operational capabilities belonged in the MVP, how the future-state platform should evolve, and how requirements could be made precise enough for engineering and testing.</p><blockquote>The product challenge was less “what features do people want?” and more “which capabilities keep the operation safe, scalable and evolvable?”</blockquote></section><section class="case-section" id="approach"><span class="eyebrow">Approach</span><h2>Translate operations into product structure.</h2><div class="decision-grid"><div class="decision-card"><span>Discovery</span><h3>Map operating needs</h3><p>Partnered with operational and business stakeholders to understand workflows, constraints and the outcomes the rebuilt system needed to support.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><span>Product definition</span><h3>Make scope explicit</h3><p>Authored features, business logic, functional requirements and acceptance criteria so scope could be challenged before it reached development.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><span>Prioritisation</span><h3>Separate MVP from horizon</h3><p>Led MVP scoping while defining a five-year roadmap, separating immediate operational necessity from longer-term product evolution.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><span>Delivery</span><h3>Keep business and engineering aligned</h3><p>Managed Agile execution across two Scrum teams, PI planning and backlog prioritisation while using delivery metrics to surface bottlenecks.</p></div></div></section><section class="case-section" id="evidence"><span class="eyebrow">Evidence</span><h2>Outputs that made the programme legible.</h2><div class="evidence-strip"><div class="evidence-pill">75 product features authored</div><div class="evidence-pill">MVP scoped</div><div class="evidence-pill">5-year roadmap</div><div class="evidence-pill">2 Scrum teams</div><div class="evidence-pill">82M+ bags / year operating scale</div></div><p>The 82M+ figure describes the operating environment. The product outputs are the discovery, feature definition, MVP scope and roadmap.</p></section><BagCustodyIllustration /><section class="case-section" id="ownership"><span class="eyebrow">Ownership</span><h2>What I owned — and what I did not.</h2><div class="ownership-box"><div><h3>I owned / led</h3><ul><li>Product discovery and MVP scoping</li><li>Feature definition and backlog prioritisation</li><li>Business logic, requirements and acceptance criteria</li><li>Roadmap definition</li><li>Agile execution across two Scrum teams</li></ul></div><div><h3>I partnered / influenced</h3><ul><li>Engineering implementation</li><li>Testing and release readiness</li><li>Operational stakeholder decisions</li><li>Vendor selection and budget alignment</li></ul></div></div></section><section class="case-section public-context" id="public-context"><span class="eyebrow">Independent public context</span><h2>The operating environment is independently visible.</h2><p class="context-intro">Public sources establish the scale and continued role of baggage reconciliation at DXB.</p><div class="source-grid"><a class="source-card" href="https://www.dnata.com/media-centre/dnata-handles-over-82-million-bags-through-dubai-international-airport-dxb-in-2022/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span class="source-type">dnata · 2023</span><strong>82M+ bags through DXB</strong><span>175km+ of baggage tracks · 100+ airlines · 253 destinations ↗</span></a><a class="source-card" href="https://www.emirates.com/media-centre/emirates-celebrates-success-of-busiest-winter-for-baggage-handling/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span class="source-type">Emirates · 2024</span><strong>BRS remains part of Dubai's baggage stack</strong><span>Dubai Airports BHS + dnata BRS + Emirates Bag Connect ↗</span></a><a class="source-card" href="https://www.iata.org/contentassets/5316edd3aafb4866876e37883211cfc4/baggage_tracking_implementation_guide.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span class="source-type">IATA · Resolution 753</span><strong>Custody tracking is an industry requirement</strong><span>Tracking points, bag inventory and handover evidence ↗</span></a></div></section><section class="case-section" id="reflection"><span class="eyebrow">Reflection</span><h2>The useful PM artifact is shared clarity.</h2><p>Large enterprise rebuilds create pressure to equate progress with feature volume. The work I value most from this programme was creating a structure where operations, engineering and delivery teams could discuss the same future state with fewer hidden assumptions.</p></section><section class="case-cta"><span class="eyebrow">Next</span><h2>Want to talk this through?</h2><div class="closing-actions"><a class="button primary" href="mailto:felipe.s.alves7@gmail.com">Email Felipe</a><a class="button ghost" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/felipealves7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a><a class="button ghost" href="/#work">More selected work</a></div></section></div></section></main>
</Base>
